    cold:"Below 30K and the labour-market crack story gets its first hard datapoint of the week. Tuesday&rsquo;s JOLTS already came in at 7.359M against a 7.400M forecast and a 7.537M prior, so the direction is established and this would confirm it." },

  { t:"2026-08-05T13:30:00Z", n:"US cash open", h:"21:30 HKT Wed", imp:3, sym:"RTY", x:1.5,
    why:"Not a release &mdash; the hour the volume arrives, and it has now arrived and been answered. Every level in this block is in CASH terms, as the whole block is. What the open was asked to settle was whether the overnight shelf was inventory or an artefact, because price had never once come back to it. It has now. RTY printed the session high at 3,050.1, which was also the top of the entire six-day measurement at the time. It then sold straight down through the shelf to 3,020.2 &mdash; beneath the whole structure rather than on it &mdash; and was trading 3,024.2 into the close of that hour. The shelf did not hold. It also did not disappear: the share of the window resting at or above 3,034.8 GREW across the break, from 11.06 per cent on the same measurement taken before the open, because the selloff traded back down THROUGH the shelf and left volume in it on the way. The live figure is on the profile card; it is deliberately not written here, because a number baked into this sentence would be a claim about a measurement that moves every refresh. That is the distinction to carry into the rest of the session. The shelf is now tested inventory that failed as support, which is a different object from untested inventory, and it is overhead rather than underfoot.",
    hotL:"The reclaim", hotD:1, coldL:"Continuation lower", coldD:-1,
    hot:"CALL &mdash; the reclaim, and it is no longer the same trade as the pullback buy. The bull case is a cash close back INSIDE the shelf, which runs 3,034.8 to 3,041.8 in cash terms. Eight bins carry 11.51% of the window between them. 3,037.8 is the heaviest of them, though no longer the cash-session point of control after the payrolls volume repriced the session &mdash; still, that is the line a reclaim has to close above rather than merely tag. WHAT WOULD BREAK IT &mdash; failing back under 3,034.8 after touching it, which converts the shelf from support that broke into resistance that held, and is the more common outcome once a level has been lost once already in the same session.",
    cold:"CALL &mdash; continuation, and the hollow is underneath now rather than ahead. Price is inside the emptiest stretch on the board: 3,010.9 to 3,033.8 is twenty-four bins with 7.04% of the window across all of them together, so there is nothing between here and the old balance to slow a second leg. The first level with real trade behind it is 2,996.9, and the shelf Monday&rsquo;s cash session built runs 2,976.0 to 2,983.9. Nine bins carry 9.55% between them, which is more than the overnight shelf holds in eight. WHAT WOULD BREAK IT &mdash; a close back above 3,034.8 on real volume, because a shelf lost and immediately regained was never a break." },

  { t:"2026-08-05T14:00:00Z", n:"ISM Services PMI · 54.5 f, 54.0 p", h:"22:00 HKT Wed", imp:2, sym:"ES", x:0.8,
    why:"Services is roughly four-fifths of the US economy, which makes this the more important of the two ISMs and the one that gets less attention. It lands thirty minutes after the cash open, so the first bar after it mixes the release with the tail of the opening auction and routinely reverses. Marked against ES rather than RTY because a broad-economy read lands cleanly on the broad index. The 50 line is not in question at 54.5; the prices-paid component is the part worth reading, because with crude back near 75 there is no commodity floor under headline inflation this week and that makes the underlying services print a cleaner signal than usual." },

  { t:"2026-08-05T20:30:00Z", n:"Trump remarks · Iran/Oman interim deal trailed", h:"04:30 HKT Thu", imp:3, sym:"ES", x:1.2,
    why:"Thirty minutes after the cash close, which is the worst hour on the clock for a headline: the cash book is shut, the futures book is at its thinnest before the Asian handover, and any move happens on spreads two to three times the cash-session width. A sixty-day interim deal over the Strait of Hormuz has been trailed for announcement. This board has no edge on the content and should not pretend to. What it can state is the mechanics: crude round-tripped from 82.25 to 74.25 on de-escalation headlines over two sessions, so the move is already substantially in the price, and the asymmetry now runs the other way &mdash; a deal that lands as trailed is close to priced, and a deal that fails to land is not priced at all.",
    hotL:"Deal confirmed", hotD:1, coldL:"No deal, or the Strait clause pulled", coldD:-1,
    hot:"Confirmation and crude stays down. That is rate relief with no growth scare, which this board has said for a month is the only configuration that lets small caps lead &mdash; and which they have now been handed twice without leading.",
    cold:"No deal, or a deal with the Strait clause pulled, and the crude premium comes back into a book that has already sold it. The gap risk is on the Thursday reopen and it is the reason a position carried over this hour is a different trade from the same position held into the close." },

  { t:"2026-08-06T12:30:00Z", n:"Unemployment Claims · 203K f, 197K p", h:"20:30 HKT Thu", imp:2, sym:"RTY", x:0.6,
    why:"Ordinarily background. It is on this card at a real band because it is the last labour datapoint before payrolls, and a claims number that breaks its range the day before NFP reliably front-runs the reaction. 197K prior is a low number by any historical standard; the forecast has it drifting up.",
    hotL:"Above 230K", hotD:-1, coldL:"Under 190K", coldD:0,
    hot:"Above 230K and the market will trade Friday&rsquo;s payrolls as a downside event before it sees them.",
    cold:"Under 190K and the labour market is still tight, which in this regime means the long end has no relief coming." },

  { t:"2026-08-07T12:30:00Z", n:"Non-Farm Payrolls · 85K f, 57K p (+ UR 4.2%, AHE 0.3%)", h:"20:30 HKT Fri", imp:3, sym:"RTY", x:2.2,
    why:"The week&rsquo;s binary, and the three numbers print in the same instant so they are one event on the tape even though the calendar lists them separately. The headline forecast has hiring recovering from a weak 57K to 85K &mdash; both figures are below the replacement rate, so this is a question about how fast the labour market is cooling, not whether. Average hourly earnings at 0.3% is the number that matters for the front end; the unemployment rate at 4.2% is the number that matters for the September meeting. Lands 20:30 HKT, an hour before the cash open, so the first move happens in futures on the widest spreads of the week. The market currently prices a greater chance of a HIKE than a cut by year-end, which is the positioning this print is landing into and the reason a soft number has more room to travel than a firm one.",
    hotL:"Hot headline, earnings 0.4%+", hotD:-1, coldL:"Soft headline, earnings 0.2%", coldD:1,
    hot:"A hot headline with earnings at 0.4% or above is the worst combination for RTY &mdash; it prices out the cut and steepens the curve at the same time. The measured downside runs through the hollow to 2,996.9, then to 2,943.2 where a sixteen-turn pool sits. Beneath it is 2,928.1, which no longer carries the point of control across all hours (it did when this branch was written) &mdash; the payrolls day moved that crown up the map.",
    cold:"A soft headline with earnings at 0.2% is the one print that lets small caps lead, and this week is the caution against assuming it does: that combination has now arrived twice from the oil side and RTY has matched the Nasdaq rather than beaten it both times. Above spot the window ends within nine bins, so there is no measured reach to quote &mdash; which is a statement about this board&rsquo;s coverage and not about the market." }
];
/* CAT-END */

/* CATR-BEGIN */
/* What the tape did after every catalyst that has already landed. Generated by
   catgen.py on every refresh; never edited by hand.

   Three things about it that the painter has to keep saying, because each one
   is a way this card could quietly lie:

   THE PROVIDER DELAY IS TAKEN OUT. A row labelled 12:18 carries an ES price
   that was true at 12:07. Read an event at 12:15 off its label and the
   "reaction" is measured entirely BEFORE the print. Every timestamp in here is
   the effective one -- when the price was true -- not the row's label.

   THE SPAN IS NOT THIRTY MINUTES. The archive steps every six, so the window
   that brackets a print runs 30 to 42 minutes wide, and each leg carries the
   span it actually measured plus a base rate computed at THAT span. A move is
   never quoted against a yardstick of a different length.

   THE TWO-HOUR NUMBER IS NOT ATTRIBUTION. At two hours the release is one of
   many things that happened, and the field is named for the window rather than
   for the event on purpose. */
