  RTY: {
    label:"RTY", unit:"pt", dp:1, seed:3022.2,
    sub:"Quoted in FUTURES, the same frame as the liquidity map below — mixing a cash ladder with a futures profile is how a level ends up eight ticks from where you think it is. The ratio on this board is 1.0024. It moved from 1.0027 on Thursday 6 August because the trigger written down here in advance on Wednesday fired, and it was executed without being re-argued under the fresh number — which was the entire purpose of writing it down. ||| Why 1.0027 became 1.0024, and why the RULE is being replaced too · three modes and a real hollow · the invalid cross-check ||| THE RULE FIRED AND THE RULE IS ALSO WRONG. Wednesday’s 20:00:00Z bar opened RTY 3,024.60 against a settled cash RUT of 3,018.4221, a ratio of 1.002047. That is a fourth consecutive step in the same direction — 1.002818, 1.002714, 1.002446, 1.002047 — and one more step down was the stated trigger, so the constant moved to 1.0024 as written. Executing it is what exposed the defect in it. The rule moves the constant to the PREVIOUS observation, so 1.0024 was already 3.5e-4 stale on the morning it was adopted, and on RUT near 3,018 that is 1.07 of a one-point RTY bin: the board adopted a constant that was already outside the tolerance the trigger is defined by. The replacement rule, for the next move on either contract, is that the constant goes to the CURRENT measurement rounded to four decimals whenever the error exceeds one bin width — not to the last one, and not on a count of consecutive steps, because a count of steps measures the trend and the thing that matters is the error. ES stays at 1.0037 and goes on the watch list rather than moving: its error grew from 6.1e-5 to 3.16e-4 in one session, which is 2.44 ES points and 0.98 of a 2.5-point bin. That is AT the threshold and not through it, and a constant re-cut every time it moves is a constant that has become noise.|Basis RE-MEASURED at Tuesday’s 20:00:00Z cash close, anchored on the OPEN of the 20:00 bar rather than its close, because that bar’s close is already the 20:05 print: RTY 3,044.50 futures against cash RUT 3,037.07, a ratio of 1.002446. Three sessions read 1.002818, then 1.002713, then 1.002446 — a compression of 3.72e-4 across three days, and accelerating: −1.05e-4 then −2.67e-4. Against 1.0027 the error is now 2.54e-4, which is 0.77 of a point on RUT at 3,037. RTY bins are one point wide, so the error is approaching one bin and has not crossed it. Re-cutting a constant every time it moves is how a constant becomes noise; re-cutting it one session too late is how a board publishes a level in the wrong bin. The rule for Thursday is written down here in advance so it cannot be argued after the fact: one more step in the same direction and the ratio moves to 1.0024. A step back toward 1.0027 and it stays.|A second estimator built from what this board called same-instant medians read 1.00248, 1.00290, 1.00276 across those three sessions and did not corroborate the trend. Two things were wrong with it and only one was known. The known one: its final reading covered ninety-nine minutes of Tuesday, because the squash truncated the quote history. The unknown one is worse, and is now measured — the legs were never same-instant. Each row of the quote archive carries one timestamp and thirteen prices at thirteen different provider delays, and here the error runs the opposite way to ES: RTY arrives 602 seconds old against cash RUT at 1,022, so this estimator was dividing a stale futures price into a staler cash one. Shifting RUT to the matching true instant walks it onto the settle-anchored number almost exactly — 3 August reads 1.002694 at a one-row shift against a bar-open 1.002714, and 4 August 1.002426 at two rows against 1.002446. The two days land at different shifts because the true offset is about one and a half rows and the archive steps only in whole ones. So the cross-check was never disagreeing with the bar-open method: it was measuring a different pair of instants and reporting the gap as a basis. It is withdrawn as evidence in both directions. Leaving 1.0027 alone rests on the three settle-anchored readings and on bin width, which is where it already rested.|Every level below is rebuilt from the same 5,715-bar, 697.6-hour, one-point-bin profile and the same session-weighted stop model the liquidity map publishes, so the two cards cannot quietly disagree. One thing this rebuild found that the last one could not say: the 70% value area is 3,001.0 to 3,061.0 and the market is not in it. 70.68% of the window traded at or beneath 2,987. The board is now describing three modes — an old value area, a hollow, and a new shelf — and the hollow is real rather than a gap in the sample.|On the reach model, the grades held their signs for a second session, which is weak evidence that they describe the model rather than only the last window: cash-hour error +12% on 126 windows against +18% last time, Asian +18% against +23%. Positive means generous, so RTY’s excursion estimates still want discounting. European hours read −21%, in the other direction, and that split is not explained. The price box takes the tape rather than a hand-typed settlement; type over it to pin the board to your own fill.",
