  HSI: {
    label:"MHI", unit:"pt", dp:0, seed:25510.33,
    sub:"READ THIS BOARD AS MHI. The levels are measured on the CASH Hang Seng, which is what the relay carries; the contract you trade is the Mini-Hang Seng future, and the basis between them is <b>not a constant</b>. Measured against real HKEX futures bars (03&ndash;07 Aug, aligned to this feed&rsquo;s cash prints inside six minutes, 123 aligned samples): the day-session MEDIAN basis ran <b>+29 (03 Aug), -5 (04 Aug), +22 (05 Aug), +15 (06 Aug), +16 (07 Aug)</b> &mdash; futures mostly above cash, wandering 35 points inside one week. At HK$10 a point, any single constant goes staler than the feed error it corrects; this card once said &ldquo;subtract six&rdquo; and that was true for one day of the five. The other caveat has NARROWED: the contract now has a real volume profile &mdash; <b>309,619</b> day-session contracts over the same span, 10-point bins, point of control <b>25,890</b>, value <b>25,600</b> to <b>26,200</b>, and the week&rsquo;s final bar printed 25,660, on value&rsquo;s lower edge with most of the week&rsquo;s trade higher. Measured 2026-08-07 from HKEX bars through a broker connection; it does not refresh with this page, so the dates are part of every claim. The LEVELS below still come from the six-minute print sample &mdash; the profile stands beside them, not underneath them. ||| The reference roll, measured &middot; why it stays on the untrusted list &middot; what would take it off ||| The previous close this card quotes is <b>25,565.74</b> &mdash; read from a quote stamped <b>Fri 28 Aug 09:45 HKT</b> (t=1787881500), which is the field this chain now keys on rather than the rebuild clock, and the last rebuild published 25,652.97, so the field HAS rolled again &mdash; the first consecutive roll recorded here. The session opened 0.61% beneath it. This sentence is written by the generator and read back by the next one, so the page is the only memory this count has. The earlier version of it claimed in this space that it therefore could not drift and could not flatter the feed. It did both, for four sessions, and the claim is struck rather than repaired: what can be said instead is narrower and checkable, which is that every increment from here has a quote stamp behind it that a reader can hold against the Hong Kong calendar.|Why it stays on the list even while the field is behaving. The reason it went on is that this defect is invisible from within a single payload, so one payload that looks right is not evidence &mdash; it is one observation of the thing that was already unobservable. The reason it stays on today is sharper and worse: the running count that was going to authorise its removal was incrementing on rebuilds rather than on rolls, so the run it had built was not evidence about Hong Kong at all. The flag survives its own removal test failing, because the test never ran. That is the honest position, and it costs the board four sessions.|What would take it off. A run of consecutive rolls long enough that a freeze would show up as a break in it, which is a claim about the next several sessions and cannot be settled on any one of them. Until then, treat every figure this feed produces as an unverified claim rather than a measurement, and note which way the error runs: a frozen reference makes a falling session look flat and a flat one look strong.",
    levels:[
      { p:26136.97, name:"The twenty-nine-day high", st:"held", tests:1,
        why:"The highest print in the whole sampled range, made on Tue 4 Aug. A held quote can miss a high but it cannot invent one, so this is the one kind of number this feed reports without qualification. The whole twenty-nine-day range beneath it is only 1,039 points wide against ES&rsquo;s 6.08% over the same window, and the tap card measures what that means: on the two clean overnight windows in the archive HSI imported 45% and then 18% of Wall Street&rsquo;s move and extended neither. It is not trending on its own account &mdash; and it is not reliably following either." },
      { p:25881.2, name:"The top of the band that held seven changes in ten", st:"held", tests:0,
        why:"Seventy per cent of every price change this feed published in the sampled range fell between this and the floor below. It is a description of a thin sample rather than a profile &mdash; no volume stands behind it &mdash; but the sample is the market's own prints, and it says the contract has spent its recent life inside a band about 478 points wide." },
      { p:25565.74, name:"The previous close", st:"untested", tests:0,
        why:"The reference every percentage on this card is computed against, which on this contract is a claim rather than a given &mdash; the disclosure above carries the running count of whether the field has been rolling. Worth watching as a level for the ordinary reason too: it is where the day's change flips sign." },
      { p:25410.46, name:"Today&rsquo;s open", st:"held", tests:1,
        why:"Where the Hong Kong session started, and within a few points of the median of every price change in the sampled range &mdash; 25,557.79. An open sitting on the middle of the recent distribution is the signature of a market with no position, which is the honest read on HSI at the moment." },
      { p:25178.0, name:"The decoupling print", st:"untested", tests:0,
        why:"Held &minus;0.11% on the day Kospi fell 11% and Shenzhen 3.4% &mdash; still the strongest decoupling print of the year, and the reason the standing call on this index has been to forecast nothing. The mechanism is Chinese firms taking share, which is bearish Korea and supportive of the China tech HSI actually holds. Far below anything the tape has touched in the measured range: context, not a level in play." },
      { p:25098.3, name:"The floor of the whole measured range", st:"held", tests:2,
        why:"The lowest the contract has been in the entire sampled range, and it has been made twice &mdash; once on Fri 14 Aug and again in the current session, a few points apart. Two visits to the same floor on different days is the closest thing to a tested level this feed can evidence, and the sampled archive never printed beneath 25,098.30." },
      { p:25000.0, name:"The line", st:"untested", tests:0,
        why:"The level that separates range from trend. Above it the decoupling long is alive; beneath it HSI is just another beta index taking its direction from the US tape, and there is no reason to hold a view on it at all. It is the one number on this board that is an argument rather than a measurement, and it is kept for that reason rather than because anything is expected to reach it soon." },
    ]
  },
  NQ: {
    label:"NQ", unit:"pt", dp:0, seed:29661.25,
    sub:"",
    levels:[
      { p:30340.0, name:"Window high", st:"untested", tests:0,
        why:"The top of the 510.2-hour window on 0.00% of window volume. Nothing above this has ever traded inside the measurement, so the correct output above it is UNMAPPED rather than clear air." },
      { p:30180.0, name:"The best bid in the twenty-eight-bin void", st:"untested", tests:0,
        why:"The heaviest bin in a twenty-eight-bin void running 29,910 to 30,180 — all of those bins together hold 10.56% of everything traded, an average of 0.38% each. This one carries 0.75% of window volume, and that is the most a retrace has to catch on anywhere inside the stretch." },
      { p:29910.0, name:"Value-area high", st:"untested", tests:0,
        why:"Upper edge of the 70% value area across the whole window, on 0.67% of window volume. 82.29% of everything traded in the window happened at or below this price, so it is the boundary value has reached rather than a shelf the market built." },
      { p:29823.2, name:"The forty-four-turn cash-made cluster", st:"held", tests:44,
        why:"Forty-four turns — fifteen in the US cash session, fifteen Asian and fourteen European — scored 38 on 0.93% of window volume, and the fourth-heaviest pool on this board. Stale at about 234 hours. The model calls its side above and grades it reachable inside a session at an excursion multiple of 3.7." },
      { p:29652.62, name:"The sixty-five-turn Asian cluster", st:"swept", tests:65,
        why:"Sixty-five turns — thirty-three Asian, seventeen in the US cash session, fourteen European and one in the evening — scored 68 on 1.21% of window volume, and the heaviest pool on this board. Set inside the last hour. The model calls its side above and grades it reachable now at an excursion multiple of 0.2." },
      { p:29600.0, name:"POC — the magnet", st:"swept", tests:46,
        why:"Point of control: more contracts changed hands in this 10-point bin than in any other across 510.2 hours, 1.75% of window volume. The eleven bins around it hold 16.45% of everything traded between them, so this is a neighbourhood rather than a line." },
      { p:29527.91, name:"The forty-four-turn Asian cluster", st:"held", tests:44,
        why:"Forty-four turns — sixteen Asian, thirteen European, thirteen in the US cash session and two in the evening — scored 52 on 1.24% of window volume, and the second-heaviest pool on this board. Set about seven hours before the snapshot. The model calls its side below and grades it reachable inside a session at an excursion multiple of 3.0." },
      { p:29410.0, name:"The top of the twenty-one-bin shelf", st:"held", tests:0,
        why:"Upper edge of a twenty-one-bin shelf running up from 29,210, and those bins hold 23.16% of everything traded between them. This bin itself carries 0.70% of window volume. Above it the profile thins." },
      { p:29286.46, name:"The thirty-two-turn Asian cluster", st:"held", tests:32,
        why:"Thirty-two turns — fourteen Asian, nine in the US cash session, six European and three in the evening — scored 26 on 1.27% of window volume. Stale at about thirty hours. The model calls its side below and grades it reachable inside a cash session at an excursion multiple of 8.5." },
      { p:29240.0, name:"Value-area low", st:"held", tests:0,
        why:"Lower edge of the 70% value area across the whole window, on 1.09% of window volume, with 88.21% of everything traded at or above it. Thin, which is what makes it a boundary rather than a shelf." },
      { p:29210.0, name:"The floor of the twenty-one-bin shelf", st:"held", tests:0,
        why:"Lower edge of a twenty-one-bin shelf running up to 29,410, holding 23.16% of everything traded between them, on 1.09% of window volume here. Under it the map is thinner in every direction." },
      { p:28940.0, name:"Window low", st:"untested", tests:0,
        why:"The floor of the 510.2-hour window on 0.02% of window volume. A market that spends six days advancing never revisits its floor, so nothing here has been tested recently enough to trust." },
